|> Section
    handle=_ch6_start_

    *What is it?*
    “Dimensional analysis” is the art of
    “checking the units” on an equation or formula
    and/or of guessing new formulas based on considerations
    about the units and/or dimensions [see below].

    !! and, 
    !! conversely,
    !! of 
    !! guessing new formulas
    !! by virtue that the units and/or
    !! dimensions should be consistent.

|> Section
    only=this
    *Terminology: “dimension” vs. “dimensional”.*
    As explained in the [Postcript to Chapter >>slopes#decoy:2](>>units_vs_dimensions_epilogue), the
    term

    __dimension__

    can mean a qualitative category of units, such as
    in the phrase “$x$ has dimensions of time”.
    On the other hand,
    !! the term

    __dimensional__

    is the adjective that means “that which relates to
    dimensions _or_ units”—as there is no special adjective
    that just means “that which relates to units”, we have this catch-all instead!
    !! (So it is.)

|> Section
    *Function signatures.*
    If $f$ is a function from $\rr$ to $\rr$
    we write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicates that inputs passed to $f$ have semantics
    of units A while outputs returned by $f$ have semantics
    of units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    indicates that $h$ 
    is a function 
    !! whose inputs 
    for which the inputs
    denote kilograms and 
    !! whose outputs 
    for which the outputs
    denote degrees fahrenheit.
    We refer to the ordered pair 
    !! of units $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ 
    !! the ordered pair 
    $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ 
    or to the notation 
    
    $$
    \Large\te{“}[\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]\te{”}
    $$
    
    as the
    _signature_
    of $f$.
    
!! |> Section

!!     *Function signatures.* 
!!     In some real-world
!!     scenarios the inputs and outputs of a function
!!     come with certain units attached. In this case,
!!     we write
!!     !! the numbers fed to a function may come
!!     !! with a certain type of unit attached, and 
!!     !! those output by the function as well; in this context, we
!!     !! write

!!     $$
!!     \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
!!     $$

!!     to indicate that the 
!!     units of inputs to $f$ are A while the units
!!     of numbers output by $f$ are B. 
!!     !! former and latter units
!!     !! are respectively A and B.
!!     !! i.e., the above indicates that 
!!     !! $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
!!     !! that takes inputs with units A
!!     !! and produces outputs with units B. 
!!     For example,

!!     $$
!!     \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
!!     $$

!!     would indicate that $h$ 
!!     is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
!!     outputs denote degrees fahrenheit.
!!     We refer to the ordered pair of units
!!     $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ 
!!     !! the ordered pair $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ 
!!     !! or to 
!!     !! !! the notation 
!!     !! or “$[\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]$”
!!     as the

!!     __signature__

!!     of the function.
!!     !! Thus a
!!     !! function signature has
!!     !! two parts: the units of the input, and the units
!!     !! of the output!

|> Section
    *Dimensionless units.* There is a special unit
    that we denote

    $$
    \Large[1]
    $$

    that stands for a “dimensionless unit” or “dimensionless
    ratio” that, conceptually, arises when a quantity
    is divided by a like-dimensioned quantity. For example,

    __radians__

    are defined as “length over length” in the $xy$-plane—specifically,
    arc length divided by radius—and are one example
    of quantities that carry units of “$[1]$”.
    The outputs of sin, cos, etc, are other examples
    of dimensionless quantities,
    being ratios of the form
    “opposite over adjacent”, etc.
    Thus
    !! it would be correct to write

    $$
    \Large \cos : [1] \ra [1]
    $$

    !! (and likewise for any other trigonometric function)
    where the first ‘$[1]$’ 
    stands for a ratio of a curved length
    divided by a straight length—the radian—and the second
    ‘$[1]$’ stands for a ratio
    of one straight length by another*—the afore-mentioned
    “opposite over adjacent”. (Or “adjacent over hypotenuse”,
    in the case of cosine!)

    |> Pause

    _*Note._ To be precise, these “lengths” are actually
    coordinate changes that may be negative—except for the
    hypotenuse, which is always positive—so they
    are “signed lengths”, or _displacements_, more accurately. 
    Also the radian carries a sign—the radian says “rotate by
    this multiple of the radius, with ‘$+$’ for counterclockwise and ‘$-$’
    for clockwise\*\*”!

    !! |> Pause

    !! _\*\*Subnote #1._ Except for the hypotenuse, which is
    !! always nonnegative! (It's “opposite” and “adjacent” that
    !! change sign as you go around the circle, not “hypotenuse”.)

    |> Pause

    _\*\*Second Note._ To get into the weeds—re: clockwise
    vs. counterclockwise—what counts as the “positive” direction
    of rotation is actually the direction _from the positive $x$
    axis to the positive $y$ axis_:

    |> Pause

    What we mean is that if it was our custom to draw the
    $y$ axis pointing downwards, but still draw the $x$ axis
    pointing rightward, the “positive” direction of rotation
    would be clockwise, not counterclockwise:

    |> Pause

    (This actually happens inside of SVG, the web-based
    standard for so-called “vector graphics”, in which the $y$
    axis points downwards; in an SVG file, 
    |> span
        class=code-cartouche
        rotate(10)
    \ will rotate an object by 10$^\circ$ _clockwise_!)

|> Section

    *Default signature.*
    For the sake of logical 
    uniformity 
    we assume that every function from $\rr$ to
    $\rr$ has a signature, where we can revert to
    ascribing the dimensionless signature 

    $$
    \Large [1] \ra [1]
    $$

    for functions for which no other information is available.

|> Section
    *Restrictions imposed on signatures by function
    operations.*
    When we add two functions, the same input is passed
    to both functions:

    $$
    \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
    $$

    This means that for the sum to make sense, $f$ and
    $g$ need to have the same units of input, i.e.,

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    would be the most general signature 
    !! pair
    that one could hope for, in order for the sum to not
    be nonsencial; but even this is too optimistic,
    since we actually need

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    in order not to add apples to oranges at the output
    end, either, 
    with “$f(x) =$ apples” and “$g(x) =$ oranges” being
    the combination to avoid. 
    !! Moreover,
    !! $$
    !! \Large f + g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    !! $$
    !! in this case.

    By contrast, when multiplying two functions we can
    stomach the output units to be different since
    composite units such as “kilowatt-hour”, “worker-years”, etc,
    are a thing, and likewise when dividing.
    !! (Mileage will vary on how meaningful these
    !! various units are, 
    !! but mathematically we don't distinguish—it is not
    !! really for us to worry about the fact that “workers times years”
    !! is more meaningful than “apples times oranges”.)
    Altogether, the following table records the various restrictions
    that apply when 
    !! adding, subtracting, etc, functions, as well 
    combining functions according to the standard
    operations, 
    as well as the signature of the 
    !! function that is created:
    function that is created in the process:

    !! Similarly other function operations such as
    !! multiplication and composition impose their own
    !! set of restrictions on the signatures, enumerated
    !! together in the following table:

    !! A bit differently, when multiplying two functions 
    !! the output units can be different since
    !! composite units such as “kilowatt-hour”, “worker-years”, etc,
    !! are a thing, and likewise when dividing two functions.

    !! Altogether, the following table records the various restrictions
    !! that apply when 
    !! combining functions according to the standard
    !! operations, 
    !! as well as the signature of the 
    !! newly created function in each case:

!! |> Section
!!     only=this
!!     *Function operations and signatures.* 
!!     Take $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$ with generic signatures $\uA \ra \uB$,
!!     $\uC \ra \uD$:

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
!!     g : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     If we try to form the sum 

!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g
!!     $$

!!     of $f$ and $g$ we find that the same input shows up as an
!!     input to both $f$ and $g$, by the definition of function addition...

!!     $$
!!     \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
!!     $$

!!     ...so taking the sum implies that

!!     $$
!!     \Large \tA = \tC
!!     $$

!!     [a.K.a.] “units of input for $f$ equals units of input for $g$”
!!     or else $f + g$ doesn't really make any sense;
!!     also,
!!     following the adage that oranges must not be added to apples,
!!     we need

!!     $$
!!     \Large \tB = \tD
!!     $$

!!     [a.k.a., “units of output for $f$ equals units of output for $g$”]
!!     or else “$f(x)$” would become the proverbial orange (apple?)
!!     and “$g(x)$” the proverbial apple (orange?); thus, altogether,
!!     !! constraints
!!     we need

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     \te{A} &= \te{C} \\
!!     \te{B} &= \te{D} \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     in order for the sum to make physical sense,
!!     !! are imposed in order for the sum to make sense, 
!!     i.e., 
!!     the pair of signatures must be of the form
!!     !! we can only take the sum $f + g$ for functions of the form
!!     !! $f$ and $g$ must have signatures of the form

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \\
!!     g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$    
    
!!     !! for some arbitrary units A and B;
!!     !! in order for the sum to make sense;
!!     for some units A, B;
!!     also note that

!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g : \uA \ra \uB
!!     $$

!!     in this case, i.e., 
!!     the signatures of $f$ and $g$ 
!!     “propagate to” or “infect” 
!!     !! (why didn't we just say “determine”???)
!!     (OK, we could just have said “determine”, but it's less dramatic)
!!     the signature of $f + g$!

!!     A little bit differently, however,

!!     __apples can be multiplied by oranges__

!!     or at least in the abstract, such as when we
!!     speak of “meters squared”, “meters per second”,
!!     “kilowatt-hours”, “worker years”, etc. This means
!!     that the product of two functions can be taken
!!     even if the output units are different. Specifically, if

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
!!     g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$   

!!     for arbitrary units A, B, C, then

!!     $$
!!     \Large fg
!!     $$

!!     is a well-formed product, and, more specifically,
!!     !! has a well-formed signature of its own, 
!!     !! with signature
!!     !! namely

!!     $$
!!     \Large fg : \uA \ra [\text{BC}]
!!     $$

!!     since in the formula “$(fg)(x) = f(x)g(x)$” for
!!     evaluating $fg$ we see
!!     that (#1) 
!!     all that we need from “$x$” in order to evaluate
!!     “$f(x)g(x)$” is to have units A, and
!!     (#2) the units of “$f(x)g(x)$” are $\tB\cdot\tC = \tB\tC$.

!!     !! Other function operations are summarized in this flower bed:
!!     !! Summarizing, and for other function operations:
!!     !! whose logic is similar:
!!     As well, differences, 
!!     quotients, and compositions of functions...

!!     $$
!!     \Large f - g
!!     $$

!!     !! and quotients of functions

!!     $$
!!     \Large f/g
!!     $$

!!     !! and compositions of functions

!!     $$
!!     \Large f \circ g
!!     $$

!!     ...only make sense when certain constraints on the
!!     signatures of $f$ and $g$ are presupposed, deduced by similar
!!     (or sometimes identical!) reasoning;  
!!     !! are all subject to similar constraints, emerging from what is
!!     !! sufficient and necessary to evaluate the operation at a given
!!     !! input; 
!!     summarizing the situation, we have...
!!     !! necessary to evaluate the formula for the operation; 
!!     !! summarizing the conclusions, that you should be able to
!!     !! reproduce yourself (!), we have...

!!     |> Pause

!!     ...the above table, that you should be able to verify
!!     for yourself.
!!     !! we duly take note of.

!! |> Section
!!     *Short form.*

!!     |> Boxed
!!         style=padding-left:1em;padding-right:1em;
!!         $$
!!         \Large
!!         \begin{gathered}
!!         (\uA\ra\uB) + (\uA\ra\uB) = \uA\ra\uB \\
!!         (\uA\ra\uB) - (\uA\ra\uB) = \uA\ra\uB \up{1.4} \\
!!         (\uA\ra\uB)(\uA\ra\uC) = \uA\ra[\te{BC}] \up{1.4} \\
