|> Section
    *Differentiation and signatures.*
    As reviewed in [Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4](>>units_of_the_derivative)
    (see also Exercise >>_complete_the_missing_units_#decoy:6 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4),
    differentiation divides the units of the output
    by the units of the input. In terms of signatures, we have

    $$
    \Large f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$

    if 
    $$
    \Large f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
    $$
    
    for any units A, B. It might also bear observing
    that this “if” is really “if and only if”—put
    differently, this means that
    
    !! It bears noting that the operation
    !! is reversible: we have

    $$
    \Large f : [\tC] \ra \left[{\tC\tD}\right]
    $$

    if

    $$
    \Large f' : [\tC] \ra [\tD]
    $$
    
    for any units C, D.
    (I.e., to recover the
    signature of $f$ from the signature of $f'$ one
    needs to multiply the output units of $f'$ by the input units
    of $f'$, while keeping the input units the same.)

|> Section
    *The differentation “operator”.*
    Hello...

|> Section
    *Differentiation and signatures.*
    As reviewed in [Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4](>>units_of_the_derivative)
    (see also Exercise >>_complete_the_missing_units_#decoy:6 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4),
    differentiation divides the units of the output
    by the units of the input: we have

    $$
    \Large f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$

    for $f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]$. Moreover, the operation
    is reversible: we have

    $$
    \Large f : [\tC] \ra \left[{\tC\tD}\right]
    $$

    for $f' : [\tC] \ra [\tD]$. (I.e., to recover the
    signature of $f$ from the signature of $f'$ you
    need multiply the output units of $f'$ by the input units
    of $f'$, while keeping the input units the same.)

    !! In other words, if

    !! $$
    !! \Large f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
    !! $$

    !! !! implies
    !! then

    !! $$
    !! \Large f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    !! $$
    !! !! $$
    !! !! \Large f' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB \over \tA}\right]
    !! !! $$

    !! !! and vice-versa: knowing the signature of $f'$ will
    !! !! also tell you the units of $f$!
    !! for any units A, B.

    !! (Specifically,

    !! $$
    !! \Large f' : [\tC] \ra \left[{\tD}\right]
    !! $$

    !! implies

    !! $$
    !! \Large f : [\tC] \ra \left[{\tC\tD}\right]
    !! $$

    !! by the same principle, but inverted—you have to
    !! multiply the output units of $f'$ by the input units
    !! of $f'$ to recover the output units of $f$!)

