    _On the topic of operators._ Hello...

!! |> Section
!!     *What is dimensional analysis?* Said topic of

!!     __dimensional analysis__

!!     is nothing more than “checking that the units
!!     work out” in an equation, i.e., that both sides
!!     of an equation carry the same units. It is a means of quickly catching mistakes
!!     but also, sometimes, of guessing a formula by virtue of knowing
!!     that the units must work out to such-and-such.

|> Section
    *Dimensional analysis of the sum rule.*
    One can check that the units check out
    (worst sentence ever) in an equation such as

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \Large (f + g)' = f' + g'
        $$

    for differentiable functions $f$, $g$,
    the _sum rule_. 
    (Exercise >>_sum_rule_summary_#decoy:14, Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4.)

    First we check that

    __the same constraints 
    on the signatures of $f$ and $g$ are imposed
    by either side of the equation__

    and then we check that, 
    for $f$ and $g$ meeting those constraints, 
    the two sides of the equation come out to the
    same units.

    On the left-hand side,

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    imposes signatures of the form

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    as listed in Table 1.1,
    while the subsequent taking of a derivative doesn't
    impose any new constraints (you can take a
    derivative no matter the signature), 
    so we find that
    $f$ and $g$ must have the same input and output
    units, as the sole constraints coming from the left-hand
    side.

    To analyze 
    the right-hand side,
    start by adopting the agnostic viewpoint that

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    for some arbitrary units A, B, C, D; then

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f' : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}/\tA] \\
    g' : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}/\tC] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    and the fact that we are taking the sum

    $$
    \Large f' + g'
    $$

    implies 

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    \tA &= \tC \\
    \tB/\tA &= \tD/\tC \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    [a.k.a., “the input units of $f'$ equal the input units
    of $g'$ and the output units of $f'$ equal the output
    units of $g'$”]
    by Table 1.1 again, which, together, imply 

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    \tA &= \tC \\
    \tB &= \tD \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...and we recover the same constraints on $f$ and $g$ as
    exist in the left-hand side, i.e., that the input units
    of $f$ equal the input units of $g$, etc!

    To continue with our program, assume now that $f$
    and $g$ have the form

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    as “required” by either side of the sum rule; then
    we deduce, one by one in order, that

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{gathered}
    f + g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    (f + g)' : [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    f' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    f' + g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    by the usual rules according to which
    signatures respond to sums and derivatives,
    but in particular

    $$
    \Large (f + g)'
    $$

    is found to have the same signature as

    $$
    \Large f' + g'
    $$

    which means that _the sum rule just possibly
    could be correct_—specifically, our
    “dimensional x-ray” of the sum rule fails 
    to uncover anything untoward!

    |> Example
        handle=_ms_x1_x2_example_
        
        If 

        $$
        \Large x_1, x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        are two differentiable functions that give 
        position-as-a-function-of-time-where-time-is-in-seconds-and-position-is-in-meters,
        i.e., two differentiable functions such that

        $$
        \Large x_1,\rt{0.2} x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        $$

        !! $$
        !! \Large
        !! \begin{aligned}
        !! f : [\te{s}] &\ra [\te{m}] \\
        !! g : [\te{s}] &\ra [\te{m}] \up{1.35}
        !! \end{aligned}
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! \Large
        !! \begin{aligned}
        !! x_1 : [\te{s}] &\ra [\te{m}] \\
        !! x_2 : [\te{s}] &\ra [\te{m}] \up{1.35}
        !! \end{aligned}
        !! $$

        where ‘s’ is seconds and ‘m’ is meters as usual,
        then

        !! $$
        !! \Large
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! \,f + g   : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \\
        !! \,(f + g)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]\up{1.35} \\
        !! \,f'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        !! \,g'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        !! \,f' + g' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35}
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        $$
        \Large
        \begin{gathered}
        \,x_1 + x_2   : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \\
        \,(x_1 + x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]\up{1.35} \\
        \,x_1'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        \,x_2'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        \,x_1' + x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        !! $$
        !! \Large f' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}],
        !! $$

        !! !! and

        !! $$
        !! \Large g' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}],
        !! $$

        !! and

        !! $$
        !! \Large (f + g)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]
        !! $$

        and, in this case, the sum rule

        $$
        \Large (x_1 + x_2)' = x_1' + x_2'
        $$

        !! (that holds if $x_1$ and $x_2$ are differentiable)
        could be verbalized by saying that

        __the rate of change of a sum of two 
        coordinates is the sum of their rates of change__

        or

        __the velocity of a sum of two points
        is the sum of their velocities__

        !! etc.
        since in the case the “rate of change” is a velocity, etc.

    |> Example

        If

        $$
        \Large x_1, x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        are given by

        $$
        \Large x_1(t) = |t| = -x_2(t)
        $$

        for all $t \in \rr$ (meaning that

        $$
        \Large x_2(t) = -|t|
        $$

        in the case of $x_2$) then $$\Large (x_1 + x_2)'$$ is defined
        at $t = 0$ but $$\Large x_1' + x_2'$$ is not. You can best see this 
        directly from the graphs:

        |> Pause

        Thus

        $$
        \Large (x_1 + x_2)' \ne x_1' + x_2'
        $$

        but the “reason”, precisely, is that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are not
        differentiable, as they
        are not differentiable, specifically, at $t = 0$.
        (But this is why we say “for differentiable $f$, $g$”
        when we present the sum rule, or other such rules.)

|> Section

    *Dimensional analysis of the “early afteroon product rule”.*
    Gottfried Liebniz, co-inventor of calculus,
    initially guessed that 

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \Large(fg)' = f'g'
        $$

    for differentiable functions $f$, $g$, an equation that
    is dubbed “the early afternoon product rule” since
    Leibniz 
    made and rectified his mistake in the span
    of one day,
    as known from his notes.

    On the left-hand side,

    $$
    \Large fg
    $$

    restricts $f$ and $g$ to have signatures of the form

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    for some units A, B, C, which is also 
    the most general type of signature that is compatible with the right-hand side—so far so good!
    !! the type of signature that the right-hand side requires—so far so good!

    However,

    $$
    \Large (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$

    whereas

    $$
    \Large f'g' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2]
    $$

    as is easy to check, and

    $$
    \Large [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2] \ne [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$

    so “the early afternoon product rule” is most definitely junk, as
    “the units don't work out”, or “it has the wrong
    dimensionality”. (I.e., the signature of $f'g'$ doesn't
    match the signature of $(fg)'$.) This is one example of
    !! a case in which 
    how
    dimensional analysis can be used to quickly
    rule out a misbegotten conjecture!
    
    !! This is one way
    !! to debunk the early afternoon product rule!

    |> Example
        handle=_second_x1x2_example_

        As in Example >>_ms_x1_x2_example_, 
        let

        $$
        \Large x_1,\rt{0.1} x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        have signature

        $$
        \,\Large x_1,\rt{0.1} x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        $$

        !! be two functions that 
        so that $x_1$, $x_2$
        give position measured in meters as a function of time measured in seconds.
        Then

        $$
        \Large x_1x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2]
        $$
        !! be two differentiable functions; 

        gives meters-squared-as-a-function-of-time-in-seconds,
        and

        $$
        \Large (x_1x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}]
        $$
        
        !! gives meters-squared-per-seconds-as-a-function-of-time-in-seconds,
        is a
        __rate of change of area [as a function of time]__
        or
        __function from seconds to meters squared per second__
        more exactly (where _meters squared per second_ are not to be confused with _meters per seconds squared_,
        that is acceleration!!), 
        whereas

        $$
        \Large x_1'x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}^2]
        $$

        has the dimensions of a

        __product of velocities__

        (which it is: $x_1'$ is a velocity,
        and $x_2'$ is a velocity), or of

        __the rate of change of a rate of
        change in area__

        (which it is not), or of
        
        __a length times an acceleration__

        (which it is not), but which
        concretely demonstrates
        the difference between the signatures of $(fg)'$ and of $f'g'$, in
        general.
        !! since $(x_1x_2)'$ has different
        !! signature from $x_1'x_2'$, they cannot possibly be equal!

        |> StarDivider

        It might be observed that
        
        $$
        \Large x_1'x_2'
        $$

        has the same dimensions (signature) as

        $$
        \Large (x_1x_2)''
        $$

        in Example >>_second_x1x2_example_—one might conjecture that

        $$
        \Large (x_1x_2)'' = x_1'x_2'
        $$

        from this observation, and one would be wrong—which is to
        show that dimensional analysis is quick to debunk an incorrect
        equation but, in the contravening case, 
        !! cannot be trusted to substitute for a real proof—however,
        cannot be trusted as a proof!—however,
        !! does not substitute for a real proof of an equation—however,

        $$
        \Large x_1'x_2'
        $$

        _does show up as a term_ in the “true” formula for $(x_1x_2)''$,
        !! in terms of $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_1'$, $x_2'$, $x_1''$ and $x_2''$, 
        !! so dimensional analysis is not _entirely_ wrong. 
        !! which is to say that 
        !! dimensional analysis doesn't go home completely empty-handed
        !! (what does this metaphor even mean), even in this case.
        !! does garner some consolation points, even in this case.
        so,
        in some sense,
        dimensional analysis is not entirely wrong,
        even in this case!

        |> StarDivider

|> Section

    *Dimensional analysis of the “late afternoon” (true) product rule.*
    The real product rule, valid for differentiable functions $f$ and $g$, is

    |> Boxed

        $$
        \Large (fg)' = f'g + fg'
        $$

    as covered in Exercise >>_product_rule_#decoy:19 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4.
    To start the dimensional analysis, we can
    !! We can
    easily see that both sides of the equation
    !! are dimensionally sound
    have sound units
    if and only if 
    $f$, $g$ have signatures
    of the form
    !! impose signatures of the form

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    in which case

    $$
    \Large (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$

    while

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{gathered}
    !! f': [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \\
    !! g' : [\te{A}] \ra [\tC/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    f'g :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \\
    fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    !! f'g + fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    and

    $$
    \Large f'g + fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    $$

    so $f'g + fg'$ has the
    same signature as $(fg)'$, i.e., dimensional
    analysis gives its _imprimatur_ to this formula!
    (Which is, indeed, the correct one.)
    !! which is, indeed, the correct formula for taking the
    !! derivative of a product of functions!

    !! which establishes that $f'g + fg'$ has the
    !! same signature as $(fg)'$, i.e., dimensional
    !! analysis gives a green light to this formula!
    !! !! does not raise any “red flags” for this equation! 
    !! (Which is indeed, the _correct_ formula.)

|> Section
    handle=chain_rule_discovery
    *Dimensional analysis and the chain rule.*
    !! We next go hunting for a formula of the form
    !! We can try finding a formula of the form
    Being bold, we can try to use dimensional
    analysis to guess a formula for

    $$
    \Large (f \circ g)'
    $$

    where $f$, $g$ are differentiable functions.

    To be clear, such a formula should use only the
    “atomic components”

    $$
    \Large f,\,g,\,f',\,g'
    $$

    on its right-hand side. These are the “lego pieces”
    from which we seek to reconstitute $(f \circ g)'$.
    !! And we hope to guess which pieces should be assembled
    !! with which based only dimensional considerations!

    (Actually, this is kind of fun to do on one's own,
    so we leave some vertical space as a “spoiler alert”, for
    those who would wish to try.)

    |> Pause

    Starting off,

    $$
    \Large (f\circ g)'
    $$

    !! only makes sense, as mentioned in Table 1.1, for 
    requires $f$ and $g$ of the form

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f &: [\tB] \ra [\tC] \\
    g &: [\tA] \ra [\tB] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    (cf. Table 1.1)
    for some units A, B, C, 
    so these are the “initial constraints” on $f$ and
    $g$. The “lego pieces” then
    !! , as well as $(f \circ g)'$, 
    have the following signatures:
    !! for some units A, B, C, in which case

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{gathered}
    f            :  [\tB] \ra [\tC] \\
    g            :  [\tA] \ra [\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    f'           :  [\tB] \ra [\tC/\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    g'           :  [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    !! (f \circ g)' :  [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    Or in more amusing pictorial form (where we
    schizophrenically put input tubes on the left, again):

    |> Pause

    From these pieces we seek to assemble a function
    of signature

    $$
    \Large [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]
    $$

    which is not a signature of any of the lego pieces;
    but since

    $$
    \Large [\tC/\tA] = [\tC/\tB]\cdot[\tB/\tA]
    $$

    we might multiply $f'$ and $g'$;
    and yet the product
    $f'g'$
    _cannot be formed_ since $f'$ and $g'$ have different
    input units!; 
    !! oh, what to do?; 
    !! the stroke of genius 
    the answer is to pre-process inputs to $f'$ via $g$,
    i.e., take
 
    $$
    \Large (f'\circ g)g'
    $$

    which makes sense since on the one hand

    $$
    \Large (f'\circ g)
    $$

    is a well-formed composition of signature

    $$
    \Large [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tB]
    $$

    whereas $g'$ has signature

    $$
    \Large [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$

    so that the product of the two has signature

    $$
    \Large [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]
    $$

    as desired! It turns out that this is the
    correct formula, i.e., 
    !! 
    we truly do have..

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \Large (f \circ g)' = (f'\circ g)g'
        $$

    ...for all differentiable functions $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$,
    a formula known as the _chain rule_.

    !! Nb: The next example, which we highly encourage
    !! you to read, will make the chain rule much, much,
    !! _|MUCH|_
    !! less abstract.

    |> Example
        Say that a

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/c6_solo_RAT.svg

        !! (yeh, yeh)
        is running a fundraising race where one function

        $$
        \Large f : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        gives money-raised-in-dollars-as-a-function-of-position-reached-in-meters,
        !! which is for all participants the same,
        whereas another function

        $$
        \Large g : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        gives position-reached-in-meters-as-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds,
        referring 
        !! to the position of this particular rat and 
        to the time elapsed since
        the start of the race.
        !! for this particular rat.
        !! (the time since the start of race, that is).
        Then

        $$
        \Large
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}] \\
        g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \up{1.35} 
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        !! $$
        !! \Large f : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}]
        !! $$

        !! and

        !! $$
        !! \Large g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        !! $$

        and

        $$
        \Large f\circ g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{\$}]
        $$

        !! where $f\circ g$ 
        gives money-raised-dollars-as-a-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds.
        !! (By this one rat.)
        Perforce,

        $$
        \Large (f\circ g)': [\te{s}] \ra [\te{\$}/\te{s}]
        $$

        gives the fundraising-rate-in-dollars-per-second-as-a-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds.

        Also note that

        $$
        \Large f' : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}/\te{m}]
        $$

        gives the
        fundraising-rate-in-dollars-per-meters-as-a-function-of-position-in-meters.
        !! dollars-per-meters as a function of position.

        Then, at $t_0$ seconds after the start of the race,

        $$
        \Large f'(g(t_0))
        $$

        is the dollars-per-meter rate at which the rat is
        fundraising, as we have fed the rat's position $g(t_0)$ into
        $f'$.

        As for the dollars-per-second rate, one can obtain
        that by multiplying the dollars-per-meter rate by the
        rat's meters-per-second velocity, i.e., by taking the product

        $$
        \Large 
        !! (f \circ g)'(t_0) = 
        f'(g(t_0))g'(t_0)
        $$

        which is indeed $(f\circ g)'(t_0)$. Per:

        |> Pause

        Also since
        $$
        \Large f'(g(t_0)) = (f'\circ g)(t_0)
        $$
        we can write the same equation as

        $$
        \Large (f \circ g)'(t_0) = (f'\circ g)(t_0)g'(t_0)
        $$

        or, abstracting the input $t_0$ away, as

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \Large (f \circ g)' = (f'\circ g)g'
            $$

        which is indeed the chain rule as we have encountered
        it before. But to recapitulate this example we have
        three different units, and three rates of change:

        $$
        \Large \left[{\te{\$}\over \te{s}}\right]\qquad\left[{\te{\$}\over \te{m}}\right]\qquad\left[{\te{m}\over \te{s}}\right]
        $$

        ...and we can obtain the former rate of change by multiplying
        together the latter two rates of change, we just have to
        be careful to evaluate $f'$ at the right “place”—we don't
        want to feed an amount of seconds into $f'$ when it is
        expecting an amount of meters!

        |> StarDivider

        _Note._ Another common way to write the chain rule is

        |> Boxed

            $$
            \Large (f \circ g)'(x) = f'(g(x))g'(x)
            $$

        which is the previous boxed equation evaluated at an input
        $x$, while using $(f' \circ g)(x) = f'(g(x))$.

    |> Example

        By [the chain rule](>>chain_rule_discovery)

        $$
        (\sin \circ \sin)' = (\cos \circ \sin)\cos
        $$

        since $\sin' = \cos$, or

        $$
        (\sin \circ \sin)'(x) = (\cos \circ \sin)(x)\cos(x)
        $$

        evaluating at an input $x$, an equation that can also be written

        $$
        (\sin \circ \sin)'(x) = \cos(\sin(x))\cos(x)
        $$

        matching the form of the boxed equation above—to be quite 
        specific, 
        e.g., 

        $$
        (\sin \circ \sin)'(12.2)
        $$
        
        which is the slope of 
        !! the rate of change of

        $$
        y = \sin(\sin(x))
        $$

        at $x = 12.2$ is

        $$
        \cos(\sin(12.2))\cos(12.2) = 0.874365...
        $$

        as a graph of $\sin(\sin(x))$ will sorta-kinda attest:

        (Because

        $$
        -1 \leq \sin(x) \leq 1
        $$
        
        the values of $\sin(\sin(x))$ are stuck between
        sine of $-1$ radian at the low end and sine of $+1$ radian
        at the high end, which produces the graph above.)


|> Section
    *Appendix: Radians _&_ the dimensionless plane.*
    Imagine that we define the radian value of an
    angle as

    __length subtended by the angle on the unit circle__

    as opposed to

    __arc length over radius__

    which is the more “traditional” definition. In 
    the second 
    case, the dimensions of the
    radian are
    
    $$
    {\te{length}\over\te{length}} = \te{dimensionless}
    $$

    whereas under the first definition it sounds like
    the dimensions are simply “length”. BUT: “length”
    _in what plane_? The “original” plane in which the
    unit circle is embedded has dimensionless axes! And
    therefore _length itself_$\rt{0.2}$ in that plane is a dimensionless
    quantity, not the usual “physical” notion of length!
    So both cases assign radians to be a dimensionless 
    unit!
    Whoo-hoo!

    Relatedly to this idea of the original “vanilla”
    $xy$-plane having dimensionless axes, you may
    have seen people label axes this way, writing
    “time/s” instead of “time $[\te{s}]$”:

    |> Pause

    The idea behind this notation is that the plane
    itself is a pristine dimensionless object, so by doing

    $$
    \Large {\te{time}\over\te{s}}
    $$

    where “time” is a time measurements in seconds,
    we have “taken the units out of the measurement”
    and obtained a “pure number” that “fits” inside
    the plane; (!); for example, this ‘$3$’ here...

    |> Pause

    ...is really a
    “$3$ without any units”
    because the
    entire plane is dimensionless, but if we follow
    the semantics of the axis label, we find...

    $$
    \Large 3 = {\te{time}\over \te{s}}
    $$

    ...which implies...

    $$
    \Large 3\te{s} = \te{time}
    $$

    ...or “time equals $3$ seconds”, i.e., that vertical
    slice of the plane corresponds to the moment when
    the “time” variable—which _does_ have units—equals
    $3$ _seconds_, not just ‘$3$’, the pure number!
